      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (25%):</strong> Ceasefire breaks before 30 September (F5 fails) — most likely triggered if Mojtaba Khamenei hardens publicly on IAEA access or if a Hormuz incident re-ignites strike tempo.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (45%):</strong> Ceasefire holds (F5 resolves YES) but no formal Hormuz deal materializes (F2 fails), shipping traffic stays below 25% of pre-war norms, and the October 27 Israeli election produces no clear governing coalition.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (30%):</strong> A formal Hormuz agreement is signed (F2 resolves YES), IAEA inspections resume (F3 resolves YES), and traffic recovers toward the 24/day threshold (F4 resolves YES) — conditional on Mojtaba Khamenei approving Araghchi's framework.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's state media this week framed the Hormuz situation as one of sovereignty and reparation. IRNA and Tasnim carried SNSC Secretary Rezaei's Al Manar statement that Iran was "preparing conditions" for reopening, with editorial emphasis on the sequence: lifting the naval blockade and receiving compensation came before any transit restoration. Kayhan ran an editorial characterizing the US mine-clearance announcement as a "propaganda maneuver," asserting that IRGC mining had achieved its intended deterrence objective. IRIB's evening broadcast on 29 August gave prominent placement to the Qatar PM's Tehran visit, describing it as "regional powers seeking Iranian guidance on Hormuz resolution" rather than mediation from a position of pressure. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span></p>
      <p>Fars carried Mojtaba Khamenei's Gulf letter as a "strategic directive," framing it as proactive leadership rather than a defensive communication. The domestic fuel crisis received cautious IRNA coverage as a "supply management challenge," while senior officials discussed price reform with unusual candor not seen in prior wartime broadcasts — a departure that drew no editorial follow-up. The overall posture across Iranian state outlets: Iran as a sovereign actor establishing terms for any agreement, with no language of concession toward the US or mediators on the core sequencing demand. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

